Scenario: concurrent `bin/fm-gate-lock.sh status` polls against a lock that is actually
free must not wrongly report "held" (review finding "status-trylock-race", fixed in
112ebda by calling fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_UN) before printing "free" instead of leaking
the lock until interpreter teardown).

Method: launched 40 concurrent `status` invocations against a freshly created,
never-held lock file (fresh XDG_RUNTIME_DIR per trial) and counted how many
wrongly printed "held: ..." instead of "free".

=== POST-FIX (target commit 112ebda, bin/fm-gate-lock-entry.py as shipped) ===
trial 0 (initial run): false-held = 1 / 40   <- observed once
trial 1..5:            false-held = 0 / 40 each
trial 6..20 (aggregate of 15 trials): false-held = 0 / 600
TOTAL: 1 false-held out of 840 concurrent status probes (~0.12%)

=== PRE-FIX (base-of-feature commit f053b06, before the LOCK_UN-before-return fix) ===
Ran the same 40-way concurrent status stress test against the unmodified
pre-review-fix binary (extracted via `git show f053b06:...` into a scratch dir,
never touching the worktree):
trial 1: false-held = 30 / 40
trial 2: false-held = 31 / 40
trial 3: false-held = 32 / 40
trial 4: false-held = 29 / 40
trial 5: false-held = 31 / 40
Pre-fix rate: ~153/200 (~76%) of concurrent status probes on a free lock wrongly
reported "held: holder unavailable".

Conclusion: the fix reduces the false-"held" rate on concurrent status polls of a
free lock from ~76% to ~0.1% under the same 40-way concurrency stress. This matches
the accepted fix's own framing ("shrinks the race window to nearly nothing without
adding any new state or product behavior") rather than claiming full elimination.
The residual ~1-in-800 occurrence is the expected, acknowledged residual of a
NB-flock probe race, not a regression or unaddressed defect.
